(The call edge is followed as far as it goes. A two-argument helper widens
 each list it writes into and leaves the others alone; a helper that hands
 its own parameter on to a second helper inherits what that one writes; and
 a call written in expression position - a declaration's initialiser, a
 `{...}` hole - carries the same verdict as a call written as a statement.
 docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #97.)

To 'note whatever' with a list called noted and a text called label.
  append label to noted.

To 'note through' with a list called noted and a text called label.
  'note whatever' of noted and label.

To 'seed both' with a list called left and a list called right and a text called label.
  append label to left,
  append 99 to right.

To 'note and count' with a list called noted and a text called label.
  append label to noted.
  Return a number, noted's length.

(Two lists, one call: each takes only the writes aimed at it.)
a list called left is [].
a list called right is [].
'seed both' of left and right and "hello".
Print "left: {left's last}".
Print "right: {right's last}".

(Through a second helper: the chain carries the write back.)
a list called relayed is [].
'note through' of relayed and "chained".
Print "relayed: {relayed's last}".

(The call as an initialiser, and the call inside a format hole.)
a list called counted is [].
a text called held is "held".
a number called 'how many' is 'note and count' of counted and held.
Print "how many: {'how many'}".
Print "counted: {counted's last}".

a list called interpolated is [].
Print "grew to: {'note and count' of interpolated and held}".
Print "interpolated: {interpolated's last}".
